=== step errors
title: When things go wrong
minutes: 20
slot: js
--- explain
Requests fail. The internet drops, or the server answers with an error **status code**: 404 (not found), 429 (too many requests), 500 (the server broke).

These are two different cases:

- **No connection:** `fetch` itself fails and **throws** an error.
- **An error status:** `fetch` works fine and gives a response, but `response.ok` is `false`.

You can throw your own error with `throw new Error("...")`. It stops the function straight away. To catch any error and handle it, use `try` and `catch`:

```js
try {
  const show = await getFavourite();   // if anything in here throws...
  console.log(show.name);
} catch (error) {
  console.log("That didn't work: " + error.message);   // ...we land here
}
```

The page has a red style for errors: add the class `error` to the status line (`.status.error`).
--- task
1. In `searchShows`, straight after the `fetch` line: if `response.ok` is false, `throw new Error("TVmaze answered " + response.status);`
2. In `loadShows`, put the `await` line and the lines after it inside `try { }`. In `catch (error) { }`, set the status text to a friendly message and add the class `error`: `statusMessage.classList.add("error");`
3. At the start of `loadShows`, remove the class again, so the next "Loading shows…" isn't red: `statusMessage.classList.remove("error");`
--- hint
`!response.ok` means "not ok". In `catch`, the status line is already showing (it says "Loading shows…"), so change its text and add the class.
--- hint
```js
async function loadShows(term) {
  statusMessage.textContent = "Loading shows…";
  statusMessage.classList.remove("error");
  statusMessage.hidden = false;
  emptyMessage.hidden = true;
  try {
    state.shows = await searchShows(term);
    statusMessage.hidden = true;
    state.genre = "";
    render();
  } catch (error) {
    statusMessage.textContent = "Sorry, the shows could not be loaded. Check your internet connection and try again.";
    statusMessage.classList.add("error");
  }
}
```
--- solution-replace
  const response = await fetch(searchUrl(term));
  const results = await response.json();
=>
  const response = await fetch(searchUrl(term));
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error("TVmaze answered " + response.status);   // stop here: we have no shows
  }
  const results = await response.json();
--- solution-replace
async function loadShows(term) {
  statusMessage.textContent = "Loading shows…";
  statusMessage.hidden = false;
  emptyMessage.hidden = true;   // an old "nothing found" doesn't belong to the new search
  state.shows = await searchShows(term);
  statusMessage.hidden = true;
  state.genre = "";   // the new shows may not have the chosen genre
  render();
}
=>
async function loadShows(term) {
  statusMessage.textContent = "Loading shows…";
  statusMessage.classList.remove("error");
  statusMessage.hidden = false;
  emptyMessage.hidden = true;   // an old "nothing found" doesn't belong to the new search
  try {
    state.shows = await searchShows(term);
    statusMessage.hidden = true;
    state.genre = "";   // the new shows may not have the chosen genre
    render();
  } catch (error) {
    // No internet, or TVmaze answered with an error: tell the person, in red
    statusMessage.textContent = "Sorry, the shows could not be loaded (" + error.message + "). Check your internet connection and try again.";
    statusMessage.classList.add("error");
  }
}
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
await t.rerun();   // start from a fresh run of the page
const s = t.$("#status");
const names = () => t.$$("#grid .card h3").map((h) => t.text(h));
const realFetch = t.win.fetch;
try {
  t.win.fetch = () => Promise.resolve(new t.win.Response("{}", { status: 500, headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" } }));
  let threw = false;
  try { await t.get("searchShows")("friends"); } catch (e) { threw = true; }
  t.expect(threw, "When TVmaze answers with an error status, `searchShows` should throw. After the `fetch` line, add `if (!response.ok) { throw new Error(\"TVmaze answered \" + response.status); }`.");
  await t.type("#search", "friends");
  await t.submit("#search-form");
  t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => !s.hidden && s.classList.contains("error"), 2000), "When TVmaze answers with an error, the page should say so in red. In `loadShows`, wrap the `await` and the lines after it in `try { }`, and in `catch (error) { }` set a message and `statusMessage.classList.add(\"error\");`.");
  t.expect(!/loading/i.test(t.text(s)), "The error is shown, but the text still says \"Loading…\". In `catch`, give `statusMessage` a friendly error message.");
  t.win.fetch = () => new t.win.Promise((resolve, reject) => t.win.setTimeout(() => reject(new t.win.TypeError("Failed to fetch")), 300));
  await t.submit("#search-form");
  t.expect(/load/i.test(t.text(s)) && !s.classList.contains("error"), "When a new search starts after an error, the loading message is still red. At the start of `loadShows`, add `statusMessage.classList.remove(\"error\");`.");
  t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => s.classList.contains("error") && !/loading/i.test(t.text(s)), 2000), "With no internet connection, the page should show your error message too. Check that the `await` line is inside `try { }`.");
} finally {
  t.win.fetch = realFetch;
}
await t.type("#search", "office");
await t.submit("#search-form");
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => s.hidden && names().includes("The Office"), 3000), "When the connection works again, a search should hide the message and show the results.");
--- learned
`response.ok` catches error answers, `throw` turns them into errors, and `try / catch` turns any error into a friendly message instead of a silent, broken page.
--- summary
Check `response.ok`, `throw new Error(...)`, and handle failures with `try / catch`.
--- mistakes
- Checking `response.ok` after `response.json()`: an error page may not be JSON, so it crashes first.
- A `catch` that does nothing: the page stays on "Loading…" forever.
- Forgetting to remove the `error` class, so the next loading message is red.
--- teacher
Students can see a real error by switching off Wi-Fi and searching (the Lab then uses sample data, so they may need to search a word that isn't in it, like "zzz", to see the empty state instead). The check simulates errors itself.


=== step no-results
title: When nothing is found
minutes: 10
slot: js
--- explain
A search that finds nothing is not an error. TVmaze answers `[]`, an empty array, with status 200 (OK). Your `renderGrid` already shows the empty message for an empty list.

Let's make the message more useful by repeating the word that was searched. Then people can spot a typo.

```js
notice.textContent = `No trains found to ${city}. Check the spelling.`;
```

Any page that loads data can be in one of **four states**: loading, error, empty, or success. Good apps show something sensible for all four. After this step, yours does.
--- task
In `loadShows`, inside `try`, before `render();`: set the text of `emptyMessage` to something like `No shows found for "zzz". Try another word.`, with the searched word (`term`) in it. Use a template literal.

Search for **zzz**, then for **dark**.
