--- learned
The page now opens with real shows, and an empty search goes back to them. data.js has done its job: tomorrow's page doesn't load it at all.
--- summary
`/shows?page=0` lists shows; `map(toShow)` converts them; `trim()` removes spaces.
--- mistakes
- `apiShows.map((s) => toShow(s.show))`: page items have no `show` inside.
- Calling `loadShows("")` above the other code, so a `const` isn't ready yet ("before initialization").
- Keeping `shows: shows`, so the sample shows flash before the real ones.
--- teacher
Online, page 0 has 250 shows, many without ratings or genres. That's why the Day 8 version of `createCard` checks for them.


=== step credit
title: Give credit to TVmaze
minutes: 10
slot: js
--- explain
TVmaze's data is free to use under a licence called **CC BY-SA**. The **BY** part means you must say where the data comes from. So every page that shows TVmaze data needs a visible link to TVmaze.

On a real website you'd type this link into the HTML. Here your code box is app.js, so you build it with JavaScript, which is good practice for links:

```js
const help = document.createElement("a");
help.href = "https://www.example.com/help";
help.textContent = "Help";
```

A link element has an `href` property, like the attribute in HTML.

`append` can also take plain text, and several things at once: `box.append(" · ", help)` adds the text, then the link.
--- task
At the bottom of your code:

1. Create an `a` element with the `href` `https://www.tvmaze.com` and the text **Data from TVmaze**.
2. Add it to the paragraph in the footer, after a separator: `document.querySelector(".site-footer p").append(" · ", credit);`

Scroll down in the preview to see it.
--- hint
Three lines to build the link (`createElement`, `href`, `textContent`) and one line to add it.
--- hint
```js
const credit = document.createElement("a");
credit.href = "https://www.tvmaze.com";
credit.textContent = "Data from TVmaze";
document.querySelector(".site-footer p").append(" · ", credit);
```
--- solution-append
// ---------- Credit: TVmaze's data is free to use if we say where it comes from ----------
const credit = document.createElement("a");
credit.href = "https://www.tvmaze.com";
credit.textContent = "Data from TVmaze";
document.querySelector(".site-footer p").append(" · ", credit);
--- check
const link = t.$$(".site-footer a").find((a) => /tvmaze\.com/i.test(a.getAttribute("href") || ""));
if (!link) {
  t.expect(!t.$$(".site-footer a").length, "There's a link in the footer, but it doesn't go to TVmaze. Set `credit.href = \"https://www.tvmaze.com\";`.");
  t.fail("I can't find a link to TVmaze in the footer. Create an `a` element, set its `href` and text, and `append` it to `document.querySelector(\".site-footer p\")`.");
}
t.expect(/^https:\/\//.test(link.getAttribute("href")), "The link should start with `https://`.");
t.expect(/tvmaze/i.test(t.text(link)), `The link text is "${t.text(link)}". Say where the data comes from, for example "Data from TVmaze".`);
--- learned
Using someone's data comes with rules. A credit link is the price of TVmaze's free API, and building it taught you `href` from JavaScript.
--- summary
Credit your data source; create links with `createElement("a")` and `href`.
--- mistakes
- Setting `credit.src` instead of `credit.href`.
- Appending to `.site-footer` instead of the paragraph inside it (works, but lands on its own line).


=== step mylist-ids
title: My List with TVmaze ids
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
My List still works, but there's a hidden problem. It stores **ids**, and it still uses the key `"myList"` from yesterday. Those old ids belong to the 12 sample shows: in the sample data, Breaking Bad is 2. On TVmaze, Breaking Bad is **169**, and 2 is a different show altogether. Old saved ids would point at the wrong shows.

When saved data changes meaning, the simplest fix is a **new key**. The old list stays where it is, unused, and the new list starts empty:

```js
localStorage.getItem("settings");     // the old format
localStorage.getItem("settingsV2");   // the new one, starts empty
```
--- task
Use the key `"myListIds"` instead of `"myList"` in both places:

1. where `state.myList` is loaded, at the top
2. in `saveMyList`

Add a show, press **Run again**, and check that the counter remembers it.
--- hint
Search your code for `"myList"` with the quotes: it appears exactly twice. Change both to `"myListIds"`.
--- hint
```js
  myList: JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("myListIds")) ?? [],
```
```js
function saveMyList() {
  localStorage.setItem("myListIds", JSON.stringify(state.myList));
}
```
--- solution-replace
  myList: JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("myList")) ?? [],   // saved ids, or an empty list
=>
  myList: JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("myListIds")) ?? [],   // saved TVmaze ids, or an empty list
--- solution-replace
  localStorage.setItem("myList", JSON.stringify(state.myList));
=>
  localStorage.setItem("myListIds", JSON.stringify(state.myList));
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
t.win.localStorage.setItem("myList", "[1,2]");
t.win.localStorage.removeItem("myListIds");
await t.rerun();
const buttons = () => t.$$("#grid .card .list-btn");
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => buttons().length === 12, 3000), "The page should load 12 shows with their + buttons. Look in the Console for an error.");
t.expect(t.text("#list-count") === "0", `The counter says ${t.text("#list-count")}: it's reading the old ids saved on Day 7. Load My List from the new key: \`localStorage.getItem("myListIds")\`.`);
await t.click(buttons()[0]);
const saved = t.win.localStorage.getItem("myListIds");
t.expect(saved !== null, "Nothing was saved under `\"myListIds\"`. In `saveMyList`, use the new key.");
t.expect(saved === "[431]", `localStorage has "${saved}" under "myListIds". After adding Friends it should be "[431]", Friends' TVmaze id.`);
t.expect(t.win.localStorage.getItem("myList") === "[1,2]", "The old `\"myList\"` key changed. Save only under the new key, `\"myListIds\"`.");
await t.rerun();
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => buttons().length === 12, 3000), "After a reload, the page should load the shows again.");
t.expect(t.text("#list-count") === "1", "After a reload the counter should still say 1. Load My List from `\"myListIds\"`.");
t.win.localStorage.removeItem("myListIds");
t.win.localStorage.removeItem("myList");
--- learned
Saved data outlives your code, so its meaning matters. A new key keeps old and new data from getting mixed up. Tomorrow My List learns to remember more than ids.
--- summary
When saved data changes meaning, save it under a new key.
--- mistakes
- Changing only one of the two keys: the list saves but never loads (or the other way round).
- A typo in one of the keys, like `"myListID"`.


=== step debounce
title: Search as you type, politely
minutes: 20
slot: js
bonus: yes
--- explain
Search-as-you-type is nice, but one request per letter is too many for TVmaze. The fix is to wait until the person **stops** typing, called a **debounce**.

`setTimeout(fn, ms)` runs a function later. It gives back a timer number, and `clearTimeout(timer)` cancels that timer before it runs.

On every letter: cancel the previous timer, then start a new one. Only the last timer survives long enough to run.

```js
let saveTimer;
notesBox.addEventListener("input", () => {
  clearTimeout(saveTimer);                          // cancel the last one
  saveTimer = setTimeout(() => saveNotes(), 500);   // try again in half a second
});
```

`let` is needed here, not `const`, because the timer changes every time.
--- task
At the bottom, add an `"input"` listener to `searchInput` that loads the shows for the trimmed text 400 milliseconds after the last letter, with `clearTimeout` and `setTimeout`. Keep the submit listener as it is.
--- hint
Declare `let searchTimer;` outside the listener, so it survives between letters.
--- hint
```js
let searchTimer;
searchInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
  clearTimeout(searchTimer);
  searchTimer = setTimeout(() => {
    loadShows(searchInput.value.trim());
  }, 400);
});
```
--- solution-append
// ---------- Search as you type, but only after a short pause ----------
let searchTimer;
searchInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
  clearTimeout(searchTimer);
  searchTimer = setTimeout(() => {
    loadShows(searchInput.value.trim());
  }, 400);
});
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
await t.rerun();   // start from a fresh run of the page
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => t.$$("#grid .card").length === 12, 3000), "The page should start with 12 shows. Look in the Console for an error.");
const names = () => t.$$("#grid .card h3").map((h) => t.text(h));
const realFetch = t.win.fetch;
let calls = 0;
t.win.fetch = (...args) => { calls++; return realFetch(...args); };
try {
  const input = t.$("#search");
  for (const v of ["f", "fr", "fri", "frie", "friends"]) {
    input.value = v;
    input.dispatchEvent(new t.win.Event("input", { bubbles: true }));
    await t.sleep(40);
  }
  const ok = await t.waitFor(() => names().length === 1 && names()[0] === "Friends", 3000);
  t.expect(calls > 0, "Typing in the search box didn't ask TVmaze. Add an `\"input\"` listener to `searchInput` that calls `loadShows` inside a `setTimeout`.");
  t.expect(ok, "After typing friends and pausing, the grid should show Friends. Call `loadShows(searchInput.value.trim())` inside the `setTimeout`.");
  t.expect(calls <= 2, `Typing five letters quickly sent ${calls} requests to TVmaze. Call \`clearTimeout(searchTimer);\` before starting the new timer, so only the last one runs.`);
} finally {
  t.win.fetch = realFetch;
}
--- learned
A debounce waits for a pause before acting. It's how search boxes on big websites stay fast without flooding their servers.
--- summary
Debounce: `clearTimeout` then `setTimeout` on every input.


=== step surprise
title: Surprise me
minutes: 15
slot: js
bonus: yes
template: extras
--- explain
The page now has a **Surprise me** button under the search box. It should pick one random show from page 0 and show only that one.

`Math.random()` gives a random number from 0 up to (not including) 1. Multiply it by the length of a list and round it down with `Math.floor`, and you get a random position in the list:

```js
const colours = ["red", "green", "blue"];
const index = Math.floor(Math.random() * colours.length);   // 0, 1 or 2
console.log(colours[index]);
```

A listener can be `async` too, so it can `await` inside.
--- task
1. At the top: `const surpriseButton = document.querySelector("#surprise");`
2. Add an `async` click listener to it that waits for `getShowsPage()`, picks one random show, puts it in `state.shows` **as a list of one** (`[show]`), sets `state.genre` to `""` and calls `render()`.
--- hint
`state.shows` must stay an array, because `render()` loops over it: `state.shows = [all[index]];`.
--- hint
```js
surpriseButton.addEventListener("click", async () => {
  const all = await getShowsPage();
  const index = Math.floor(Math.random() * all.length);
  state.shows = [all[index]];
  state.genre = "";
  render();
});
```
--- solution-replace
const statusMessage = document.querySelector("#status");   // Day 8: "Loading..." and errors
=>
const statusMessage = document.querySelector("#status");   // Day 8: "Loading..." and errors
const surpriseButton = document.querySelector("#surprise");
--- solution-append
// ---------- Surprise me: one random show from page 0 ----------
surpriseButton.addEventListener("click", async () => {
  const all = await getShowsPage();
  const index = Math.floor(Math.random() * all.length);
  state.shows = [all[index]];   // still a list, with one show in it
  state.genre = "";
  render();
});
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
await t.rerun();   // start from a fresh run of the page
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => t.$$("#grid .card").length === 12, 3000), "The page should start with 12 shows. Look in the Console for an error.");
await t.click("#surprise");
const ok = await t.waitFor(() => t.$$("#grid .card").length === 1, 3000);
if (!ok) {
  const st = t.get("state");
  t.expect(!(st && st.shows && !Array.isArray(st.shows)), "`state.shows` must stay a list. Put the random show inside square brackets: `state.shows = [all[index]];`.");
  t.fail("Clicking **Surprise me** should leave exactly one card. Add an `async` click listener to `surpriseButton` that picks one show from `await getShowsPage()` and calls `render()`.");
}
t.expect(typeof t.get("state").shows[0].name === "string", "The random item should be a show from `getShowsPage()`.");
--- learned
`Math.random()` and `Math.floor` pick a random item. And listeners can be `async`, so a click can wait for data.
--- summary
`Math.floor(Math.random() * list.length)` picks a random position.


=== step result-count
title: How many shows?
minutes: 10
slot: js
bonus: yes
template: extras
--- explain
The toolbar now has an empty line, `<p class="meta" id="result-count">`. Fill it with the number of shows on the page, like "12 shows".

Where should that line go? Everything on the page is drawn by `render()`, and `render()` knows the final `list`, after the genre filter. So that's the place: every time the page is drawn, the count is drawn too.

```js
counter.textContent = `${items.length} items in your basket`;
```
--- task
1. At the top: `const resultCount = document.querySelector("#result-count");`
2. In `render()`, after the genre filter, set its text to the number of shows in `list`, followed by " shows".

Click a genre chip and watch the number change.
--- hint
Use `list.length`, not `state.shows.length`: the list after the genre filter is what's on the page.
--- hint
```js
  resultCount.textContent = `${list.length} shows`;
```
--- solution-replace
const statusMessage = document.querySelector("#status");   // Day 8: "Loading..." and errors
=>
const statusMessage = document.querySelector("#status");   // Day 8: "Loading..." and errors
const resultCount = document.querySelector("#result-count");
--- solution-replace
  renderGrid(list);
  renderGenres();
=>
  resultCount.textContent = `${list.length} shows`;
  renderGrid(list);
  renderGenres();
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
await t.rerun();   // start from a fresh run of the page
t.expect(await t.waitFor(() => t.$$("#grid .card").length === 12, 3000), "The page should start with 12 shows. Look in the Console for an error.");
t.expect(/\b12\b/.test(t.text("#result-count")), `The count line says "${t.text("#result-count")}". In \`render()\`, set \`resultCount.textContent\` to \`list.length\` and " shows".`);
await t.click(t.$$("#genres button").find((b) => t.text(b) === "Comedy"));
t.expect(/\b4\b/.test(t.text("#result-count")), `After choosing Comedy, the count says "${t.text("#result-count")}". It should say 4: use \`list.length\` after the genre filter, not \`state.shows.length\`.`);
--- learned
Anything that depends on the state belongs in `render()`. Then it's always right, whatever changed.
--- summary
Counts and labels that depend on the state are drawn in `render()`.
